        "text": "{\"scores\": {\"heroes\": 3, \"craft\": 3, \"facts\": 2, \"age_fit\": 4, \"continuity\": 4, \"delight\": 3}, \"evidence\": {\"heroes\": \"Lennon drives Levels 1-4 and the finale: the volley through the cuju hole, the rules vote, and the first-ever penalty all use his penalty practice. In Level 6 he says \\\"Not on my watch\\\" and then just watches the match, and in Levels 7-8 he unwraps a bundle or stands in a highlight reel while Bit explains. Roblox never shapes anything, and the shootout game he is building gets only one passing mention.\", \"craft\": \"The structure is strong: a cold open of backyard penalties, a clear device (the glowing ball through the fence), return home, and a pixel-message callback. But challenges fade after Level 4, Level 8 is a flashing fact list, and the unexplained 'deleter' never becomes a real antagonist. The last line is printed twice (\\\"Saturday couldn't come soon enough.\\\" appears before and after 'The End').\", \"facts\": \"Three core dates are wrong and repeated in the True Facts list: the Football Association was founded in 1863, not 1847; the penalty kick entered the laws in 1891, not 1911; the first World Cup was in 1930, not 1926. The newspaper 'typo' tale from 1950 is a popular legend presented unhedged, and the net with a hole belongs to later Song-era cuju, not '2,000 years ago'. Many other facts are correct: Pickles, Gaetjens, Dick, Kerr Ladies, the 1921-1971 ban, the Ken Aston traffic-lights idea, the 2-1 half-time score, Chastain, Messi.\", \"age_fit\": \"At 2,633 words the story is inside the target range. Vocabulary and peril (the mob stampede, dodged into a hay cart) suit a 10-year-old, and there is some age-appropriate wit. The humour leans more silly than dry for band C, and the passing mention of people 'betting England will win by ten' sits awkwardly with the gambling guidelines for a children's story, though it stops short of promoting gambling.\", \"continuity\": \"Bit, the golden coins fusing into a trophy, \\\"PLAYER ONE\\\", \\\"BZZZT\\\", \\\"Level complete\\\" and the watery pixel eyes are all reused correctly. The opening refers back to the video-game adventure but not to the last ending (THE NEXT LEVEL IS YOURS TO BUILD / a game to make). Bit makes no 8-bit jokes, and the title recycles the previous title rather than building on it.\", \"delight\": \"There are genuinely fun beats: the goose, the grumpy \\\"...aye\\\" in the hacking vote, and Pickles wiggling his whole body. But much of the middle is Bit lecturing, the finale leans on stock phrasing (\\\"the whole world exploded into color\\\"), and the duplicated closing line would trip a parent reading aloud.\"}, \"caps_applied\": [\"none\"], \"disqualified\": false, \"overall\": 3.05, \"editor_notes\": [\"Fix the three wrong dates (FA 1863, penalty kick 1891, first World Cup 1930) in both the story and the True Facts list. Hedge the 1950 newspaper 'typo' legend in-story (\\\"people like to say…\\\") and move the net-with-a-hole detail to the later Song-era game.\", \"Give Lennon a real problem to solve in Levels 6-8, for example reading the 1950 game like a coach, or deciding which highlight to rescue first. Tie one challenge to his Roblox shootout game, such as spotting a 'bug' in the level the way he would when building his own.\", \"Delete the duplicated \\\"Saturday couldn't come soon enough.\\\" after 'The End'. Swap the betting line for a non-gambling way to show England were favourites, and add a couple of Bit's 8-bit jokes to replace some of the lecturing.\"], \"best_moment\": \"\\\"No kicking people in the shins?\\\" A long pause. Then, grumpily: \\\"...aye.\\\"\", \"worst_moment\": \"Lennon cracked his knuckles. \\\"Not on my watch.\\\"\"}"
